a) Simplified snowflake schema
A snowflake schema organizes data into a central fact table connected to normalized dimension tables.
FACT_SALES
Product_ID, Customer_ID, Date_ID, Region_ID, Quantity, Revenue
DIM_PRODUCT
Product_ID, Category_ID
DIM_CUSTOMER
Customer_ID, Age_Group
DIM_DATE
Date_ID, Month, Year
DIM_REGION
Region_ID, Region_Name
The product dimension may link to a separate category dimension, and the region dimension may link to a geography dimension, forming a snowflake structure.
The fact table stores numerical sales measures, while dimension tables store descriptive information for analysis.
b) ETL process
ETL stands for Extract, Transform, and Load.
1.	Extract: Collect sales data from CSV files, operational databases, and other sources.
2.	Transform: Remove duplicates, handle missing values, standardize dates, validate product IDs, and calculate revenue.
3.	Load: Load dimension tables first, then insert sales records into the fact table.
4.	Validate: Check record counts, totals, and referential integrity.
c) OLAP operations
i) Roll-up: Summarizes detailed sales information into a higher-level group, such as total sales by region.
SELECT r.Region_Name,
       SUM(f.Revenue) AS Total_Revenue
FROM FACT_SALES f
JOIN DIM_REGION r
ON f.Region_ID = r.Region_ID
GROUP BY r.Region_Name;
ii) Dice: Selects a subset of data based on multiple dimensions, such as age groups and product categories.
SELECT c.Age_Group,
       p.Category_ID,
       SUM(f.Revenue) AS Total_Revenue
FROM FACT_SALES f
JOIN DIM_CUSTOMER c
ON f.Customer_ID = c.Customer_ID
JOIN DIM_PRODUCT p
ON f.Product_ID = p.Product_ID
WHERE c.Age_Group IN ('18-25', '26-35')
  AND p.Category_ID IN (1, 2)
GROUP BY c.Age_Group, p.Category_ID;
The example assumes that these age-group labels and category IDs exist in the dataset.
d) Business insights
•	Regions with high sales may receive more inventory.
•	Regions with low sales may need targeted promotions.
•	Age-group analysis helps identify customer preferences.
•	Category-level analysis helps with stock planning.
•	Historical sales trends support future demand forecasting.
